# CHANGELOG

## 20260823

0.14.0: + a date line carrying more than one date, the last being the completion date.

1. + expand_dates(): a header's date line may carry several dates, the work having spanned days, and only as much as changes is written. An element escalates against the one before it: one or two digits a day within the same month, four MMDD within the same year, eight a date outright, and a line may escalate mid-way — `20091218, 19, 20100112`. The last is the completion date, which is the one a version takes.
2. ~ parse_date(): `/\A# (\d{8})\s*\z/` took a lone date and nothing else, so every multi-date header wrote ???????? into its heading and the date had to be supplied by hand afterwards. A retired form, but the headers carrying it are not going to be rewritten: it is the record of when the work happened.
3. Run against the Browser library's sixteen revisions, three headings move from ???????? to a date: 20221205, 20230102 and 20250925, the last from `20250922, 23, 24, 25`. git-import-all parses fifteen entries where it parsed fourteen, an entry having been merged into its neighbour for want of a heading to separate them.
4. + two examples, run to 17, both failing against 0.13.0.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.13.0/0.14.0/


0.13.0: + a library's header, read from lib/ where there is no bin/, and --header-file naming one outright.

1. + find_library(): a library keeps its header in lib/, named for the project, where a program carries one alone in bin/. Preferred is the file named for the revisions root, matched without regard to case; failing that the only .rb there, two being nothing to choose between. Until now changelogger read bin/ or nothing, so every library fell to tier 2 and was written out as `## ????????` with no version, no summary and a bare list of files changed.
2. + -f, --header-file: the header named outright, relative to each numbered directory. It settles which file is read, which is the only way a project holding both bin/ and lib/ can say which it means, and it covers a library whose name is not the revisions root's.
3. ~ find_binary(): the switch first, then bin/, then lib/. bin/ before lib/ keeps every project which already worked reading exactly what it read before.
4. No new dependency: the switch is read from ARGV rather than through switches.rb, this program having carried no require at all and its formula no resource.
5. + four examples, run to 15. Against 0.12.1 the two library ones fail and the other two pass, so the first two catch this and the second two guard what was already right — the bin/ preference, and the section header which 0.12.1 fixed and nothing had covered.
6. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.12.1/0.13.0/


0.12.1: A section header ends the Changes section whether or not text follows its colon.

1. ~ parse_changes(): a section header stopped the Changes section only when nothing followed its colon, the test being `/\A# [A-Z][A-Za-z ]*:\s*\z/`. `# Todo:` matched and stopped it. `# History: I realised when wanting to dump all bookmarks...` did not, so that paragraph and everything below it was read as part of the Changes section and written out as unparsed `?` items, four of them in every entry the section reached. Anchoring upon the colon alone stops both.
2. Met upon the Browser library, whose lib/Browser.rb carries Changes, then History, then Todo. changelogger cannot read a library's header at all — it looks in bin/ — so this was reached by putting that header where bin/ is looked for, which is what 0.13.0 is for.
3. Nothing which already worked moves: run before and after against dump_browser_history_to_csv and open_browser_history_from_csv, sixteen revisions between them, the CHANGELOG written is identical either way. The eleven examples pass.
4. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.12.0/0.12.1/


0.12.0: + test/, MiniTest spec style, running against revisions built for each example.

1. + test/changelogger_test.rb: eleven examples, each building its own numbered revisions in a temporary directory and reading the CHANGELOG which was written into them. The revisions root is the whole of the interface and was already there, so nothing needed adding to the program to make it testable. Modelled upon audit_requires' test, which builds a gem home per example for the same reason: what is installed upon this machine, or staged beside it, decides nothing.
2. + CHANGELOGGER, an environment variable naming the binary under test, so that the suite can be pointed at another revision. Run against 0.10.1 two of the eleven fail — the two written for the fault 0.11.0 fixed — which is how they are known to catch it rather than merely to pass here.
3. The other nine pass against 0.10.1, pinning behaviour which was already right: tier 1 taking a Changes section as written, tier 3 inventorying the first revision, methods added and removed and altered, the cumulative order, the skip which leaves a hand-written entry alone, and the name change tier 2 cannot see from structure. Guards rather than regressions, and worth saying which is which.
4. No Rakefile, the tap's other programs having none and this being run as `ruby test/changelogger_test.rb`. moby carries one because moby is a gem.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.11.0/0.12.0/


0.11.0: Read a method's end as its own, a modifier having been counted as opening a block.

1. ~ extract_method_body(): the depth count took any line holding `if`, `unless`, `while`, `until`, `do`, `def`, `class`, `module`, `case`, `for` or `begin` as opening a block. A modifier opens nothing and carries no end — `return nil if x` and `depth = n if line =~ //` are ordinary Ruby and appear throughout — so the count never came back down and the extraction ran past the method's own end into the rest of the file. tap-audit's install_body is sixteen lines and was read as six hundred and eighty, ending at `main`.
2. + BLOCK_OPENERS, + BLOCK_DO, + opens_block?(), + closes_block?(): a keyword opens a block where it begins the expression, which is what the line-start anchor says, and `do` where it ends the line, with or without its parameters. A `do` elsewhere upon the line belongs to a one-line block, which closes itself.
3. What this was doing to the record: every method holding a modifier was reported as altered whenever anything below it changed, tier 2's `~` items being the only thing which reads a body. Run across tap-audit's thirteen revisions before and after, the count went from 802 to 181, so 621 were false. The `+` and `-` items are unaffected and were identical across all thirteen either way, those being read from the sets of names rather than from any body.
4. It is the tier which needs the most editing afterwards which was the least trustworthy. Anything generated by tier 2 and edited into a repository carries this: gem-homepage's CHANGELOG was written that way, and moby's backfill below 0.7.0 may have been.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.10.1/0.11.0/, and the date.


## 20260821

0.10.1: + LICENSE, which the README has named without one being present.

1. + LICENSE: the MIT text, copyright 2026 thoran. The year is that of the first commit. The README has answered "MIT" under a heading of its own while the repository carried no licence text at all, so anyone taking it at its word had nothing to read and nothing to comply with.
2. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.10.0/0.10.1/, and the date.


## 20260821

0.10.0: Write one date heading for the versions which share it.

1. ~ format_entry(): returns the date beside the body rather than writing it into the body, so that the writer decides when a heading is due.
2. ~ write_changelog(): a heading only where the date differs from the one above it. Entries stay two blank lines apart whether or not a heading stands between them.
3. ~ CHANGELOG: one heading per date, thirteen becoming four.
4. ~ README.md: say that a heading is written once for the versions which share it, and that git-import-all reads that form as of 0.14.0.
5. Across the twenty repositories the tap installs from, 293 headings state a date where 168 occasions had one. git-import-all's 20140510 stated its date fourteen times.


## 20260807

0.9.0: Join an item continued on the next line.

1. + continuation_line?(): True where the entry has an item, the line is indented past where an item's number would sit, and it does not begin with an enumerator. An item too long for its line is continued on the next; should2expect writes three that way, and they were being reported as not understood, the item's meaning split between the item and a mark.
2. ~ parse_changes(): + a branch joining such a line onto the last item with a space between.
3. The enumerator guard is what tells a wrapped line from a sub-item. exitor's i to iii under its item 9 begin something of their own rather than continuing it, and are still reported rather than being run together into a single line, which leaves the choice where it belongs.
4. ~ README.md: Say that an item may be continued on the next line.


0.8.0: Take a prose line where a summary belongs as the summary.

1. + summary_line?(): True where the entry has neither a summary nor any items yet, and the line does not begin with a digit. The line after the section's heading, or after a marker carrying no summary of its own, is that entry's summary. startor writes every one of its entries that way and had five of them reported as not understood.
2. ~ parse_changes(): + a branch taking such a line as the summary, beginning the entry where the section has not begun one.
3. The two guards are what keep it from over-reaching. An entry which already has items is past the place a summary belongs, so exitor's sub-items are still marked rather than swallowed. Prose does not begin with a digit, so should2expect's "0: Handle verbs ending in y.", which is a marker written the wrong way, is still marked rather than taken for a summary and quietly half-corrected.
4. ~ README.md: Say where a summary may be written.


0.7.0: Take a Changes section carrying no marker as one entry.

1. + begin_entry(): Creates an entry and appends it to the list, both the marker branch and the item branch now needing to.
2. ~ parse_changes(): An item met before any marker begins an entry of its own, rather than being dropped by the "&& current_entry" guard, which is gone. A marker tells one version's items from another's within a cumulative section, so a section covering a single version needs none and a good many carry none: jira's 0.2.0 and 0.3.0, startor's first entry, should2expect's whole section. They were reported as not understood at best, and lost at worst.
3. ~ parse_changes(): Lines met before any entry began attach to the first of them, so that they surface when that entry is the one returned. Without this the orphans went missing the moment the items began an entry of their own, which is the thing 0.6.0 exists to prevent: should2expect's "0: Handle verbs ending in y." went again without a word.
4. ~ README.md: Say that a section covering a single version carries no marker.


0.6.0: Mark what the Changes section holds and cannot be parsed, rather than dropping it.

1. + unparsed_items(): A warning on stderr naming the revision and the line, and an item reading "? <line>" in the entry, for whatever the section holds which is none of a marker, a numbered item or a blank line. It follows heading_date(), which does the same for a missing date: warn, leave an obvious hole, and let the run finish rather than stopping it.
2. ~ parse_changes(): + an else, so that a line matching no branch is collected rather than falling through the loop unremarked. + @unparsed_lines.
3. ~ process_initial_version() and process_subsequent_version(): Append the marks to the items. In the latter the collection is taken before changes_are_new?(), which parses the previous version and would otherwise leave that one's lines standing in place of this one's.
4. + UNPARSED_MARK
5. The three shapes which prompted this, none of them worth a branch of its own: exitor's sub-items numbered i, ii and iii; should2expect's marker written "0:" rather than "-/0"; startor's summary written with no marker above it. Eleven lines across the three, every one lost without a word. Worse than the count suggests: should2expect's marker not being recognised left current_entry nil, so the guard on the item branch dropped its three items too, the section came back empty, and the version silently took a structural diff in place of the entry sitting in its header.
6. The lines attach to the entry current when they are met, and orphans, met before any marker, to the run. The Changes section is cumulative across a minor series, so a version's header carries the earlier markers' lines as well as its own, and holding them all against the returned entry put exitor's 0.2.0 sub-items into the entries for 0.2.1 and 0.2.2 too, three versions reporting the same three lines.
7. ~ README.md: Say what happens to a line which cannot be parsed.


0.5.2: Take the parentheses off a marker's summary.

1. + marker_summary(): Strip a wrapping pair of parentheses, and return nil where nothing is left. The older headers write the summary as "-/0 (Text.)" and the newer ones as "-/0: Text.", and the parentheses are the marker's punctuation rather than part of what it says. exitor's entries were coming out as "0.2.2: (Detect full country names correctly.)" against a commit message of the same line without them.
2. ~ parse_changes(): Use marker_summary(), which also absorbs the empty-summary check that followed the assignment.


0.5.1: End the Changes section at any heading, not at one of four named ones.

1. ~ parse_changes(): /(Discussion|Notes|Todo|Examples):/[A-Z][A-Za-z ]*:/, anchored to the end of the line so as it matches a heading and not an item which happens to contain a colon. Markers, numbered items and blank lines are all matched before this branch is reached, so the only lines it can see are headings.
2. The four named sections were a closed list against an open set. gem-homepage's header says "Note:", singular, and so was not ended: its numbered line was taken as an item and written into the entry for 0.2.0. Usage: and Description: appear in other repositories and would have done the same.
3. ~ README.md: Say that any further heading ends the section, rather than naming four.


0.5.0: Report a change of the program's name, which the structural diff could not see.

1. + parse_name(): The program's name from the header, told from the date and the version by their leading digit. It mirrors parse_version() and parse_date(), which already read the same block for the two fields below it.
2. + name_change(): /old/new/ where the two differ, in the notation the changelogs already use, and first among the items, a change of identity leading whatever else the version did. Found while backfilling git-checkout-new-issue, whose 0.1.0 renamed the program from git-checkout_with_jira and left no other trace: the file was already bin/git-checkout-new-issue, so only the header changed and tier 2 reported nothing at all of it.
3. ~ extract_structure(): + name. A name declared in the header is a declaration as much as a require or a def is, and the structure hash is what tier 2 compares.
4. ~ structural_diff(): Begin the items with name_change().
5. ~ empty_structure(): + name, so that a file which is not there compares as having no name rather than as having none of a key.
6. inventory_items() is left alone, so tier 3 is unchanged. A first version does not change its name, and reporting it there would say only that the program is called what it is called.
7. ~ README.md and the Tiers section of the header: Say that tier 2 reports a change of name.


## 20260806

0.4.1: Document the Changes section which tier 1 reads.

1. ~ README.md: + The Changes section, being cumulative across one minor series and restarted at each new one so that it cannot grow without bound, what "Changes since 0.5:" names, what the -/0 and n/n+1 markers mean and why the dash, that items are numbered continuously across the whole section, that only the last marker's items become the entry, and what ends the section. It was the least recoverable part of the format, being visible in the headers but nowhere stated.
2. ~ README.md: Name the tiers where they are described. The script header calls them tiers, and the README twice refers to them by number, but the description itself never introduced the term.


0.4.0: Write the CHANGELOG in the form the repositories already use.

1. ~ format_entry(): A "## <date>" heading, with the version and summary on the first line of the body and the items unindented, in place of a "<version> - <date>" heading with indented items. git-import-all reads this directly, so the output no longer wants converting by hand before an import.
2. ~ write_changelog(): Two blank lines between entries rather than one, so that the boundaries stay visible where items run to several sentences.
3. + heading_date(): Warn on stderr naming the revision, and write ???????? where the script header carries no date, rather than inventing a date or stopping the run. The output is a seed to be edited, so an obvious hole in it is honest.
4. + summary_line()
5. - version_label(): The -/0 and n/n+1 markers exist because a Changes section spans a whole minor series, restarted at each one to keep it from growing without bound. Once every version has its own dated entry there is nothing left for them to disambiguate. They are still parsed out of the headers, merely no longer written.


0.3.0: Add a README and a TODO, and retire the Changes section from the script header.

1. + README.md: What the tool is for, the three tiers and the order they are tried in, the skip on an existing CHANGELOG, the output layout, the relationship to git-import-all and git-boot, and a note on this repository's own CHANGELOG having been seeded by the tool.
2. + TODO.txt: The four items which had been kept as a loose "changelogger roadmap" file alongside the revisions, where git-import-all would have taken it for a revision of its own had it not begun ignoring everything but numbered directories.
3. - The Changes section from the script header. The CHANGELOG holds that record now, and keeping both invites them to disagree. Revisions 0 to 2 keep theirs, being the source the CHANGELOG was seeded from.


